# 001 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-001", "Cities and geography",
   "Closing a shopping street to cars tends to help its trade, but only where customers can already reach it without driving.",
   "Easy",
   "Shopkeepers often resist plans to close a street to cars, fearing that customers who cannot park outside will go elsewhere. In many towns where such schemes have gone ahead, the fear has proved exaggerated: people on foot linger, return more often and spend more over a month than drivers who stop briefly, and footfall on the closed street tends to rise. Yet the gains are not automatic. A pedestrian street works when it already sits within walking distance of homes, offices or public transport, so that people arrive on foot anyway. Where a high street serves mainly shoppers who drive in from scattered suburbs, closing it to traffic without offering another way to reach it can empty it, and the shopkeepers' fear turns out to be justified.",
   "Closing a shopping street to traffic tends to help its trade, but only where enough customers can reach it on foot or by public transport.",
   [("Closing shopping streets to cars always increases trade, because shoppers on foot linger longer and spend more than drivers, so shopkeepers' fears are unfounded.",
     "The paragraph says 'the gains are not automatic' and that for car-dependent high streets 'the shopkeepers' fear turns out to be justified', so 'always' and 'unfounded' contradict its qualification."),
    ("Shoppers who arrive on foot tend to return more often and spend more over a month than drivers, who usually stop at shops only briefly.",
     "This repeats one supporting detail from the second sentence and leaves out the central claim and its condition about how customers reach the street."),
    ("Towns should build large car parks beside pedestrian streets so that suburban drivers can still reach the shops after traffic is banned.",
     "The paragraph mentions 'another way to reach it' only as a condition and never recommends car parks; the option adds a prescription the paragraph does not make.")],
   "The paragraph corrects the shopkeepers' fear (pedestrian streets usually gain trade) and then limits the correction: it holds only where the street is 'within walking distance of homes, offices or public transport'. The correct answer keeps both the claim and the condition.",
   "Taking the first half as the whole point and ignoring 'Yet the gains are not automatic'.",
   "V02H-pedestrianised-streets-retail",
   "The qualification is stated in one plain sentence beginning 'Yet', and each distractor is clearly off once it is read.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# 002 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-002", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Blaming by outcome conflicts with the control principle, but applying that principle strictly would leave little to blame.",
   "Medium",
   "Two drivers take the same corner too fast after the same glass of wine; one reaches home, the other strikes a cyclist who appears suddenly from a side lane. Most people blame the second driver far more, even though the two drivers made identical choices and differ only in what happened next, which neither controlled. This reaction sits uneasily with the widely held principle that people should be judged only for what is within their control. If we applied that principle strictly, we would have to say the drivers are equally culpable, and that our sharper condemnation of the second is a kind of error. The difficulty is that almost everything we do has consequences shaped by circumstance, so a strict rule of control would leave very little that anyone could be blamed or praised for at all.",
   "Judging people by outcomes they did not control clashes with the principle of judging only what is controlled, yet applying that principle strictly would leave almost nothing to blame.",
   [("Since the two drivers made identical choices, our harsher condemnation of the one who struck the cyclist is simply an error that careful moral reasoning ought to correct and remove.",
     "The paragraph says only that the strict principle 'would' force this verdict, and then raises 'The difficulty' with that principle; it does not endorse calling the reaction an error."),
    ("Because consequences are always shaped by circumstance, nobody can ever be held morally responsible for anything they do, whether it turns out well or badly.",
     "The paragraph says a strict rule of control 'would leave very little' to blame; it presents this as a problem for the rule, not as a conclusion that no one is ever responsible."),
    ("Drinking before driving is wrong whatever happens on the road, and drivers who take corners too fast deserve blame even when nobody is hurt at all.",
     "The example is used to test how we assign blame; the paragraph makes no argument about the wrongness of drink-driving, so the option is out of scope.")],
   "The paragraph sets an intuition (blame the driver who struck the cyclist more) against a principle (judge only what is controlled), and then shows that resolving the conflict in favour of the principle has a heavy cost. The point is the unresolved tension, which the correct answer states with both sides.",
   "Reading the paragraph as siding with the control principle, when its last sentence turns against applying it strictly.",
   "V02H-moral-luck",
   "One distractor states the conditional verdict as the author's conclusion; the solver must notice the final 'difficulty' that keeps the tension open.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# 003 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-003", "Psychology",
   "Knowing the outcome makes it look predictable, which leads to unfair judgements of past decisions.",
   "Easy",
   "After learning the outcome of an event, people find it remarkably hard to recover how uncertain it looked beforehand. The outcome reorganises our memory of the evidence: signs that pointed towards it now seem obvious, while signs that pointed elsewhere fade or are reinterpreted as noise. As a result we tend to believe that we, and others, should have seen it coming. This matters most when we judge decisions. A doctor whose patient suffered a rare complication, or a manager whose project failed, is likely to be faulted for missing warnings that were, at the time, mixed in with many others pointing the opposite way.",
   "Knowing an outcome makes it seem more predictable than it was, so we judge past decisions too harshly for missing signs that were unclear at the time.",
   [("Doctors and managers are often blamed for failures that resulted from rare complications or unforeseeable events rather than from any error in their own decisions.",
     "The doctor and the manager are examples; the paragraph's point is a general distortion of memory caused by knowing the outcome, which this option leaves out."),
    ("People who know how an event turned out tend to forget the outcome and remember instead the warning signs that pointed in other directions.",
     "This reverses the paragraph, which says signs pointing towards the outcome 'now seem obvious' while those that 'pointed elsewhere fade'."),
    ("Since no outcome can ever be predicted in advance, decision makers should never be held responsible when the results of their choices turn out badly.",
     "The paragraph claims only that outcomes look more predictable afterwards than they were; it neither says nothing is predictable nor proposes that no one be held responsible.")],
   "The paragraph explains a bias (the outcome 'reorganises our memory of the evidence') and its main consequence (unfair judgement of decisions). The correct answer states both.",
   "Picking the option built from the examples instead of the general mechanism.",
   "V02H-hindsight-bias",
   "The mechanism and its consequence are stated directly; the distractors are a detail, a reversal and an overstatement.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# 004 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-004", "Society and culture",
   "Wedding spending is an investment in standing rather than mere imprudence, though competition makes it a burden on the poor.",
   "Medium",
   "Lavish weddings are often criticised as a simple failure of prudence: families spend their savings, or borrow, for a single day that leaves nothing behind. The criticism misreads what the money buys. In many communities a wedding is the occasion on which a family displays its standing to a wide network of relatives, neighbours and future in-laws, and that standing affects the marriages its other children can make, the help it can call on and the credit it is offered. Seen this way, the spending is less a lapse than an investment in reputation. None of this makes it harmless. When every family must match its neighbours, the display that once signalled standing becomes a cost that all must pay merely to avoid falling behind, and the people least able to afford it are pressed hardest.",
   "Heavy wedding spending is better seen as an investment in social standing than as mere imprudence, though competition can make it a burden falling hardest on poorer families.",
   [("Lavish weddings are a wasteful habit in which families recklessly give up their savings and take on heavy debt for one celebration that leaves them with nothing of lasting value.",
     "This is the criticism the paragraph says 'misreads what the money buys'; it is reported in order to be corrected, not endorsed."),
    ("Families who spend heavily on weddings are making a sensible investment, since the reputation they gain brings them better marriages, more help and easier credit.",
     "This keeps the reinterpretation but drops the qualification 'None of this makes it harmless' and the point that competition presses the poorest hardest."),
    ("Communities should agree to limit wedding spending so that poorer families are no longer forced to borrow simply in order to keep up with richer neighbours.",
     "The paragraph diagnoses a competitive burden but proposes no remedy; the agreement to limit spending is a prescription it does not make.")],
   "The paragraph rejects the 'failure of prudence' reading, reinterprets the spending as an 'investment in reputation', and then qualifies that the competition makes it a cost 'all must pay', heaviest on the poor. The correct answer holds all three moves.",
   "Choosing the option that agrees with the reinterpretation but ignores the qualification that follows it.",
   "V02H-wedding-spending-status",
   "The tempting distractor matches the paragraph's main turn; it is wrong only because it omits the final qualification.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# 005 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-005", "History and politics",
   "Structures set the range of outcomes; how much an individual mattered depends on how wide that range was.",
   "Difficult",
   "It has become routine to dismiss the history of great individuals as naive, on the ground that leaders merely ride forces (economic, demographic, technological) that would have produced much the same results without them. The correction was needed, but it has been pushed too far. Structural forces set the range of what is possible; they rarely settle which possibility is realised. At moments of crisis, when institutions are weak and outcomes hang on a few decisions, the character and judgement of particular people can tip events one way or another, and the effects can last for generations. The sensible position is not to return to heroic biography but to ask, case by case, how wide the range left open by circumstance actually was, since that width determines how much any individual could matter.",
   "Structural forces limit what can happen but seldom fix the outcome, so an individual's influence depends on how much room circumstances left, which tends to be widest in crises.",
   [("History is driven chiefly by the character and judgement of exceptional leaders, whose decisions at moments of crisis shape events far more than economic or technological forces do.",
     "The paragraph explicitly declines 'to return to heroic biography' and says structural forces 'set the range of what is possible', so it does not rank leaders above structures."),
    ("Leaders largely ride economic, demographic and technological forces, which would have produced broadly similar historical outcomes whatever particular individuals happened to be in charge.",
     "This is the dismissive view the paragraph says 'has been pushed too far'; it is reported in order to be corrected."),
    ("Individuals shape history only during crises, while in ordinary times structural forces alone decide which of the possible outcomes is actually realised in the end.",
     "The paragraph says structures 'rarely settle which possibility is realised' in general and asks 'case by case' how much room was left; 'only' and 'alone' turn its emphasis on crises into a rule it never states.")],
   "The paragraph corrects an overcorrection: structures bound the possible but 'rarely settle' the outcome, and the room left to individuals varies, being largest in crises. The correct answer keeps the general principle, the variable room and the role of crisis without making crisis the sole condition.",
   "Selecting the 'only during crises' option because crises are the paragraph's illustration, missing that the paragraph treats room for individuals as a matter of degree in every case.",
   "V02H-great-man-history",
   "Two distractors are the two positions the paragraph sits between, and the third hardens a matter of degree into an absolute; separating them needs the whole argument.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=D)

