ps("V02-H-007", "Cities and geography",
   "Port-city openness comes from the practical needs of trade, which is why it can fade when trade declines.",
   "Medium",
   "Port cities have long been more open to strangers than inland towns of similar size, and the usual explanation credits the sea with broadening minds. The cause is more practical. A port lives by trade with people it cannot choose, and its merchants, dock workers and officials learn that a ship's crew from a distant coast must be housed, fed, paid and understood if business is to continue. Tolerance there begins as a working habit, enforced by self-interest, before it becomes a value anyone defends. This also explains why such openness can be fragile. When trade declines or shifts to other harbours, the practical reason for accommodating outsiders weakens, and port cities have sometimes turned as suspicious of strangers as any inland town.",
   "Port cities are more open to strangers because trade demands it, not because the sea broadens minds, which is why that openness can fade when trade declines.",
   [("Living beside the sea broadens the outlook of a city's inhabitants, which is why port cities have long been more welcoming to strangers than inland towns are.",
     "This is 'the usual explanation' that the paragraph sets aside with 'The cause is more practical'."),
    ("Port cities welcome foreigners only out of self-interest and never come to regard tolerance as a value that is worth defending for its own sake.",
     "The paragraph says tolerance 'begins as a working habit ... before it becomes a value anyone defends', which implies it can become such a value; 'never' contradicts this."),
    ("Merchants and officials in ports must house, feed, pay and understand foreign crews, since otherwise the trade on which their city depends would stop.",
     "This restates one supporting detail and omits both the contrast with the usual explanation and the point about fragility.")],
   "The paragraph replaces the romantic explanation (the sea broadens minds) with a practical one (trade forces accommodation) and draws a consequence: openness built on trade 'can be fragile'. The correct answer states the cause and the consequence.",
   "Choosing the 'only out of self-interest' option, which sounds like the paragraph's practical explanation but overstates it.",
   "V02H-port-cities-cosmopolitanism",
   "The extreme distractor echoes the paragraph's emphasis on self-interest; it fails on 'before it becomes a value', which takes a careful reading.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

