ps("V02-H-008", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "A justified political wrong still wrongs someone: justification overrides a claim without cancelling it.",
   "Difficult",
   "It is sometimes said that a politician who lies to the public or bargains with a ruthless faction to prevent a greater harm has simply done the right thing, and that once the calculation favours the act, no moral residue remains. This tidy view misdescribes the situation. The act may be justified, all things considered, and still involve doing something wrong: deceiving people who were owed the truth, or lending strength to people who deserved none. A leader who felt nothing after such a choice would be more worrying than one who felt the weight of it, because that weight is how a person registers that a real claim was overridden rather than dissolved. Justification answers whether one should act; it does not erase what the act cost those it wronged.",
   "A politician may be right overall to deceive or compromise for a greater good yet still have wronged someone, since justification overrides a real claim without cancelling it.",
   [("Once a politician's lie or compromise is justified by preventing a greater harm, it is simply the right thing to do and leaves nothing behind to regret.",
     "This is the 'tidy view' that the paragraph says 'misdescribes the situation', because it denies any 'moral residue'."),
    ("Politicians who deceive the public or bargain with ruthless factions act wrongly whatever their reasons, because no greater good can ever justify overriding the real moral claims of other people.",
     "The paragraph grants that 'The act may be justified, all things considered'; it denies only that justification erases the wrong, so this option reverses its concession."),
    ("Leaders who feel the weight of their hard choices are more trustworthy than those who feel nothing, since remorse is a reliable sign of good political character.",
     "The paragraph uses the leader's feeling as evidence that 'a real claim was overridden'; it says nothing about trustworthiness or character in general, so the option shifts the point to a side remark.")],
   "The paragraph rejects the view that justification leaves 'no moral residue' while keeping the claim that the act 'may be justified'. Its point is the distinction in the last sentence: justification settles whether to act, not whether anyone was wronged. The correct answer states both halves.",
   "Treating the paragraph's rejection of the tidy view as a claim that such acts are never justified, or mistaking the remark about feeling for the main point.",
   "V02H-dirty-hands-political-ethics",
   "The correct answer must be told apart from a reversal that also stresses the wrong; the solver must hold 'may be justified' and 'does not erase' together.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=D)

