ps("V02-H-009", "Psychology",
   "Later work suggests passivity under stress is the default and that what is learned is control.",
   "Medium",
   "Early laboratory work on helplessness suggested a simple lesson: animals exposed to shocks they could not escape learned that nothing they did mattered, and later failed to escape even when escape became easy. The idea spread quickly into accounts of human depression and of children who stop trying at school. Later research reversed the emphasis. Passivity in the face of prolonged stress, it now appears, is the default response, and what is learned is the opposite: that one's actions can make a difference. Experiences of control teach organisms to override the default. The practical implication shifts accordingly. Rather than looking only for the damage done by uncontrollable events, one should ask whether a person has had enough experiences of mastery to learn that effort pays.",
   "Later work suggests passivity is not learned from uncontrollable events but is the default response to stress, and what experiences of control teach is that action helps.",
   [("Animals and people repeatedly exposed to events that they cannot control learn that their own actions are useless, and so they stop trying even when escape becomes easy.",
     "This is the 'simple lesson' of the early work, which the paragraph says later research 'reversed'."),
    ("Depression in adults and lack of effort among schoolchildren are both caused mainly by too few experiences of mastery during the early years of childhood.",
     "The paragraph mentions depression and schoolchildren only as places where the early idea 'spread'; it makes no causal claim about either and says nothing of early childhood."),
    ("Prolonged stress is what teaches organisms that their efforts make a difference, which is why passivity appears only when such stressful experiences are absent.",
     "The paragraph says passivity under 'prolonged stress' is the default and that 'Experiences of control' teach efficacy; the option garbles the relation between stress and learning.")],
   "The paragraph reports a finding (helplessness is learned) and then its revision: passivity is 'the default response', and what is learned is 'that one's actions can make a difference'. The correct answer states the revision, which is the paragraph's point.",
   "Choosing the early finding because it is described in the most detail.",
   "V02H-learned-helplessness",
   "The solver must see that the first half of the paragraph is superseded; the option repeating it is detailed and plausible.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

