ps("V02-H-010", "Society and culture",
   "Authentic food cannot mean one original recipe, but it can mean a cook's understanding of a tradition.",
   "Medium",
   "Diners often praise a restaurant for serving the authentic version of a dish, meaning the one that matches how it is made in its place of origin. The standard is harder to apply than it sounds. Most celebrated dishes have changed repeatedly, absorbing ingredients that arrived through trade or conquest, and cooks in the same region disagree about the correct recipe. What is called authentic is usually one moment in that history, often the version a visitor first encountered, fixed and treated as the original. This does not make every claim of authenticity empty. A cook who learned a tradition from the inside and respects its logic is doing something different from one who merely borrows a name; but the difference lies in that understanding, not in matching a single correct recipe.",
   "Food authenticity cannot mean fidelity to one original recipe, since dishes keep changing, but it can still mark a cook's real understanding of a tradition rather than mere borrowing.",
   [("Since every celebrated dish has changed over time and cooks disagree about recipes, the very idea of authentic food is meaningless and ought to be abandoned by diners altogether.",
     "The paragraph says 'This does not make every claim of authenticity empty', so calling the idea meaningless contradicts its qualification."),
    ("Many famous dishes have absorbed ingredients brought by trade or conquest, so the recipes now served in their places of origin are themselves fairly recent.",
     "This takes one supporting detail and adds a claim about recipes being 'fairly recent' that the paragraph does not make; it misses the point about what authenticity can mean."),
    ("A dish is authentic when it is cooked exactly as the people of its region made it at the moment a visitor first tasted it there.",
     "The paragraph describes this as the mistake: one moment 'fixed and treated as the original'. The option presents the error as the definition.")],
   "The paragraph criticises the recipe-matching notion of authenticity and then rescues a different notion: understanding a tradition 'from the inside'. The correct answer states the critique and the rescue.",
   "Stopping at the critique and choosing the option that throws the idea away.",
   "V02H-food-authenticity",
   "The extreme distractor follows the paragraph's critique faithfully until the last sentence reverses it.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# 011 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-011", "History and politics",
   "Overextension is applied after the fact; loyalty of elites and revenue capacity explain imperial survival better than size.",
   "Medium",
   "A familiar account of why great empires fall points to overextension: they conquer more territory than they can defend or administer, and the cost of holding distant frontiers drains the centre. The account is attractive because it turns decline into a lesson about limits. Its weakness is that it is applied after the fact. Empires that collapsed are described as having overreached, while others that held equally vast and costly territories for centuries are not, and the difference is rarely explained by size alone. What seems to matter more is whether the centre could keep the loyalty of provincial elites and raise revenue without provoking revolt, capacities that some large empires possessed and some small ones lacked.",
   "Overextension explains imperial collapse less well than it seems, being invoked only after the fact; keeping provincial elites loyal and raising revenue peacefully matter more than an empire's size.",
   [("Great empires fall because they conquer more territory than they can defend or administer, so that the cost of their distant frontiers gradually drains the centre.",
     "This is the 'familiar account' whose weakness the paragraph exposes: it 'is applied after the fact' and does not explain why equally large empires survived."),
    ("The size of an empire has no bearing on its survival, which depends entirely on the loyalty of its provincial elites and the willingness of its subjects to pay taxes.",
     "The paragraph says the difference is 'rarely explained by size alone' and that loyalty and revenue 'matter more'; 'no bearing' and 'entirely' overstate a comparative claim."),
    ("Modern states should learn from the fall of empires that expanding beyond their means invites collapse, and should keep their commitments within their capacities.",
     "The paragraph draws no lesson for modern states, and it doubts the 'lesson about limits' that this option recommends.")],
   "The paragraph critiques a popular explanation on two grounds (it is applied only after collapse, and size does not separate failures from survivors) and offers an alternative: elite loyalty and the capacity to raise revenue. The correct answer keeps the critique and the alternative while keeping the comparative 'matter more'.",
   "Accepting the absolute option because it names the alternative factors, without noticing that the paragraph only ranks them above size.",
   "V02H-imperial-overextension-decline",
   "The extreme distractor names the right factors but changes 'matter more' into 'entirely'; the solver must catch the degree.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# 012 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-012", "Economics and business",
   "Mission statements tell outsiders something only when they rule out choices a competitor might make.",
   "Easy",
   "Almost every company now publishes a mission statement, and most of them sound alike: commitments to customers, integrity, innovation and people. Because the words cost nothing and exclude nothing, they tell an outsider very little about how the firm will behave. A statement becomes informative only when it rules something out, when it commits the company to a choice that a competitor might reasonably make differently, such as refusing a profitable line of business or accepting slower growth for some stated reason. Statements of that kind are rare precisely because they constrain the people who write them, and that constraint is what gives them meaning.",
   "Most mission statements reveal little because they commit firms to nothing; a statement means something only when it rules out choices a reasonable competitor might make.",
   [("Mission statements are a useless corporate ritual, and companies would lose nothing by abandoning them altogether and simply describing the products and services they sell instead.",
     "The paragraph says a statement 'becomes informative' when it rules something out, so it does not call all mission statements useless or propose abandoning them."),
    ("Most corporate mission statements sound much alike, promising customer focus, integrity, innovation and care for people, because firms tend to copy their competitors' wording.",
     "This repeats the opening observation and adds a cause, copying, that the paragraph never gives; it misses the point about what makes a statement informative."),
    ("Firms that refuse profitable business or accept slower growth for stated reasons are more ethical than competitors whose mission statements promise nothing specific.",
     "The paragraph is about whether a statement carries information, not about which firms are more ethical; the comparison is outside its scope.")],
   "The paragraph argues that cheap, inclusive wording conveys nothing and that a statement gains meaning only by constraining the firm. The correct answer states that criterion.",
   "Choosing the 'useless ritual' option because the paragraph is critical of most statements.",
   "V02H-corporate-mission-statements",
   "The criterion is stated in one sentence ('informative only when it rules something out'); the distractors are clearly off once it is read.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# 013 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-013", "Cities and geography",
   "Planned capitals are judged unfairly early, but their thin street life is partly fixed in their layout.",
   "Difficult",
   "Capitals built from scratch, on open land chosen by the state, are usually judged against old capitals that grew over centuries, and they usually lose: their avenues are wide and empty, their ministries far apart, their street life thin. The comparison is partly unfair. An old capital is the survivor of many cities that never grew; the planned capital is judged in its first decades, before people have had time to adapt it to their purposes. Yet the unfairness cuts only so far. The features that make a planned capital feel lifeless (the separation of government from commerce, a scale designed for ceremony rather than walking) are fixed in its layout, and time can fill the spaces between buildings without changing how far apart they are.",
   "Planned capitals are judged too early and against survivors, yet their thin street life is partly built into layouts that time can fill in but cannot fundamentally change.",
   [("Planned capitals compare poorly with old capitals only because they are judged in their first decades; given enough time, they will develop an urban life every bit as rich as that of old capitals.",
     "The paragraph says 'the unfairness cuts only so far' because the lifeless features 'are fixed in its layout'; promising a life 'every bit as rich' ignores that limit."),
    ("Since the layouts of planned capitals are fixed, comparisons with old capitals are entirely fair, and their lifelessness is permanent whatever people later do with them.",
     "The paragraph calls the comparison 'partly unfair' and says time 'can fill the spaces between buildings', so both 'entirely fair' and 'permanent whatever people later do' contradict it."),
    ("Old capitals are lively because they grew over centuries alongside commerce, while planned capitals fail because the state chose open land far from existing cities.",
     "The paragraph locates the problem in layout (separation of functions and ceremonial scale), not in the choice of site, and the option drops the point that the comparison is partly unfair.")],
   "The paragraph makes a concession and then limits it. The concession: planned capitals are judged against survivors and too early. The limit: their lifeless features are 'fixed in its layout'. The correct answer keeps both, including the precise point that time can fill gaps but not move buildings.",
   "Choosing the option that stops at the concession, since the paragraph spends several sentences defending planned capitals before turning.",
   "V02H-planned-capital-cities",
   "Two distractors each keep one half of a concession-and-limit structure, and the third misplaces the cause; the correct answer is fixed only by holding both halves.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=D)

# 014 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-014", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Moral deference troubles us because moral belief should rest on one's own understanding, not because no experts exist.",
   "Difficult",
   "We defer to experts on bridges and medicines without hesitation, yet many people feel that deferring to someone else on a moral question (adopting a view on the ethics of eating meat simply because a thoughtful philosopher holds it) would be a failing. One explanation is that there are no moral experts to defer to. That seems too quick: some people have plainly thought harder and more carefully about such questions than others. A better explanation is that moral belief is meant to be connected to one's own understanding of why something is wrong; a person who refrains from cruelty only on someone's say-so seems to have the right belief in the wrong way. On this account deference may sometimes be prudent, when one must act and cannot work the question through, but it cannot be the end of the matter.",
   "Moral deference troubles us not because moral expertise is impossible but because moral belief should rest on one's own understanding, so deference can be a stopgap, never a final answer.",
   [("People rightly hesitate to defer to others on moral questions, because unlike engineering or medicine, ethics has no experts whose judgement is any better than anyone else's.",
     "This is the 'One explanation' the paragraph calls 'too quick', since 'some people have plainly thought harder and more carefully' about moral questions."),
    ("Since moral belief must rest on one's own understanding, it is always wrong to adopt any moral view on the authority of another person, however thoughtful or well informed that person may be.",
     "The paragraph allows that 'deference may sometimes be prudent, when one must act'; 'always wrong' contradicts that concession."),
    ("Moral experts deserve the same deference we give engineers and doctors, since some people have thought harder about ethics than others and so are more likely to be right.",
     "The paragraph grants that moral expertise exists but argues that deferring to it differs from deferring on bridges, because belief should be 'connected to one's own understanding'.")],
   "The paragraph rejects one explanation of our unease about moral deference (no experts exist), offers another (moral belief should be grounded in one's own understanding), and limits it (deference may be prudent when one must act). The correct answer states all three.",
   "Choosing the 'always wrong' option, which follows the paragraph's preferred explanation but ignores its final concession.",
   "V02H-deference-to-moral-experts",
   "The paragraph weighs two explanations and then concedes a limit; each distractor captures one of these moves in isolation and the strongest one fails only on 'always'.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=D)

# 015 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-015", "Psychology",
   "Cohesion alone does not cause bad group decisions; its combination with a directive leader or isolation does.",
   "Medium",
   "Close-knit teams are often blamed for poor decisions, on the theory that members who like one another suppress doubts to preserve harmony. Yet many of the most effective teams are also highly cohesive, and their members argue fiercely because they trust that disagreement will not cost them their place. Cohesion, it seems, is not the culprit by itself. The trouble comes when cohesion is combined with a dominant leader who signals the preferred answer early, or with isolation from outside views, so that loyalty to the group comes to mean agreeing with it. Breaking up close teams to prevent bad decisions would therefore sacrifice the trust that makes candid disagreement possible.",
   "Cohesion alone does not cause poor group decisions; harm comes from combining it with a dominant leader or isolation, and breaking up close teams would destroy the trust behind candid dissent.",
   [("Members of close-knit teams suppress their doubts in order to preserve harmony, which is why such teams tend to make poorer decisions than looser and less friendly groups of colleagues do.",
     "This is the theory the paragraph reports and then rejects with 'Cohesion, it seems, is not the culprit by itself'."),
    ("Highly cohesive teams always make better decisions than loose ones, because members who trust one another feel free to argue fiercely about every question they face.",
     "The paragraph says cohesion causes trouble when 'combined with a dominant leader' or 'isolation', so 'always' better contradicts it."),
    ("Leaders should avoid stating their preferred answer early in a discussion, since doing so leads loyal team members to agree with them instead of openly voicing their own doubts.",
     "The dominant leader is one of two conditions the paragraph names; it gives no advice to leaders, so this option is both narrow and a prescription it does not make.")],
   "The paragraph critiques the view that cohesion causes groupthink, locates the harm in cohesion combined with other conditions, and concludes against breaking up close teams. The correct answer states the diagnosis and the conclusion.",
   "Picking the advice to leaders, which sounds like a sensible lesson from the paragraph but is not its point.",
   "V02H-groupthink-cohesion",
   "The reported view is described in detail first, and a narrow prescription is plausible; the solver must follow the paragraph's turn at 'Yet'.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# 016 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-016", "Society and culture",
   "Fashion changes because styles stop being distinctive once they spread, not because they stop being attractive.",
   "Easy",
   "Fashion moves in cycles because it serves two opposed wishes at once: to resemble the people one admires and to stand apart from the people one does not. A style adopted by a small, admired group spreads as others imitate it; but the more widely it spreads, the less it distinguishes anyone, and the group that began it abandons it for something new. The style then lingers among late adopters until it seems dated, and it becomes available for revival only once it has been forgotten long enough to look fresh. The engine of change is therefore social rather than aesthetic: styles are dropped not because they stop being attractive but because they stop being distinctive.",
   "Fashions change because people want both to imitate admired groups and to stand out, so a style is dropped once its spread means it sets nobody apart.",
   [("Styles go out of fashion when people stop finding them attractive, and they return only once tastes have changed enough for them to look appealing to people again.",
     "The paragraph says styles are dropped 'not because they stop being attractive but because they stop being distinctive', which this option reverses."),
    ("Out-of-date styles linger among late adopters until they look dated, and they can be revived only after being forgotten long enough to seem fresh again.",
     "This restates one stage of the cycle described in the third sentence and leaves out the two opposed wishes that drive it."),
    ("Fashion is controlled by small, admired groups who deliberately invent new styles every season in order to stay ahead of the many people who keep imitating them.",
     "The paragraph says the admired group abandons a style once it spreads; it says nothing about control, deliberate invention or seasons, so the option adds an outside idea.")],
   "The paragraph explains the fashion cycle by two opposed wishes (imitation and distinction) and concludes that change is 'social rather than aesthetic'. The correct answer states the mechanism and its consequence.",
   "Choosing the option about revival, which is accurate but describes only the tail of the cycle.",
   "V02H-fashion-cycles-imitation",
   "The final sentence states the point outright, and the distractors are a reversal, a detail and an addition.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# 017 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-017", "History and politics",
   "Revolutions follow improvement because discontent tracks the gap between expectations and reality.",
   "Medium",
   "It seems natural to expect revolutions where misery is deepest, yet they have often broken out in societies that were growing richer and whose rulers had begun to reform. The puzzle dissolves once one considers what people compare their situation with. Long and settled hardship tends to produce resignation, because it is all that people expect. When conditions start to improve, expectations rise faster than conditions can, and when a reforming regime then stalls or reverses course, the gap between what people now think possible and what they actually have becomes intolerable. Discontent, on this view, tracks the distance between hopes and reality, not the absolute level of hardship.",
   "Revolutions often follow improvement rather than settled misery because rising conditions raise expectations, and unrest grows from the gap between those hopes and what people actually have.",
   [("Rulers who wish to avoid revolution should never begin to reform, since any improvement in their subjects' conditions will inevitably provoke a rebellion against them.",
     "The paragraph explains a pattern; it gives no advice to rulers, and it says unrest follows when reform 'stalls or reverses course', not that any improvement 'inevitably' provokes rebellion."),
    ("Revolutions break out where misery is deepest and most prolonged, since the people who have suffered longest and worst have the strongest reasons to overthrow their rulers.",
     "This is the 'natural' expectation the paragraph corrects; it says long hardship 'tends to produce resignation'."),
    ("People who have lived through long and settled hardship usually become resigned to it, because such hardship is all that they have ever learned to expect.",
     "This is one step in the explanation; it leaves out the central claim that discontent tracks 'the distance between hopes and reality'.")],
   "The paragraph poses a puzzle (revolutions in improving societies) and resolves it: rising conditions raise expectations, and a stalled reform opens an intolerable gap. The correct answer states the pattern and its explanation.",
   "Choosing the prescription for rulers, which seems to follow from the paragraph but overstates it and ignores the role of a stalled reform.",
   "V02H-revolutions-rising-expectations",
   "The prescription distractor is a tempting overextension of the explanation, and the detail about resignation is accurate but partial.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# 018 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-018", "Economics and business",
   "Remittances help households in ways aid does not, but cannot replace public investment and may weaken pressure for reform.",
   "Medium",
   "Money sent home by migrants now reaches many poor countries in larger sums than foreign aid, and it has advantages aid lacks: it goes directly to households, it tends to rise when the home country suffers a disaster, and it pays for food, school fees and medical care that families choose for themselves. It is tempting to conclude that remittances can do the work of development. The conclusion overreaches. Remittances smooth and raise household consumption, but they do not build roads, courts or power grids, and a country that comes to depend on exporting its workers may find that its most enterprising people, and the political pressure they might have exerted for reform, are abroad.",
   "Remittances help poor households more directly than aid and hold up in crises, but cannot supply public infrastructure or institutions, and reliance on emigration may drain pressure for reform.",
   [("Because remittances now exceed foreign aid and reach households directly, they can replace aid as the main engine of development in many poor countries.",
     "This is the conclusion the paragraph calls tempting and then says 'overreaches', since remittances 'do not build roads, courts or power grids'."),
    ("Remittances harm poor countries, since they drain away their most enterprising workers and lead families to spend on consumption instead of building the roads and power grids the country needs.",
     "The paragraph lists real advantages of remittances and never says they harm countries or that families should build roads; it says only that remittances do not supply such public goods."),
    ("Migrants tend to send more money home after a disaster, which makes remittances a more dependable source of support in hard times than foreign aid is.",
     "This picks out one advantage from the first sentence and omits the paragraph's main point, that remittances cannot 'do the work of development' because they do not build public goods and may export would-be reformers.")],
   "The paragraph sets out the advantages of remittances, rejects the inference that they can 'do the work of development', and gives two reasons: they do not fund public goods, and dependence on emigration may export reformers. The correct answer holds the advantages and both limits.",
   "Choosing the option that treats the paragraph's criticism as a claim that remittances do harm, when it says they help households.",
   "V02H-remittances-development",
   "The 'harm' distractor uses the paragraph's own words about enterprising people and roads but reverses its overall assessment.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# 019 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-019", "Cities and geography",
   "Gentrification may not push many residents out, yet it excludes poorer newcomers, transforming areas gradually.",
   "Difficult",
   "When a poor neighbourhood starts to attract wealthier residents, the common fear is that existing tenants are driven out. Some careful tracking of households has found something less dramatic: long-standing residents of gentrifying areas move away at rates not much higher than those in similar neighbourhoods that are not changing, and those who stay benefit from safer streets and better shops. This finding is real but easy to misread. It concerns whether people leave, not who arrives. As rents rise, the poorer households who would have moved in, replacing those who leave for ordinary reasons, can no longer afford to, so the neighbourhood is transformed over a decade without any dramatic eviction. Displacement, in other words, may happen less at the door than in the queue for vacant homes.",
   "Gentrification may not push out many existing residents, yet it can shut out poorer newcomers who can no longer afford to move in, transforming an area gradually.",
   [("Fears about gentrification are largely mistaken, since long-standing residents rarely leave changing neighbourhoods and those who stay go on to enjoy safer streets and better shops.",
     "This stops at the finding that the paragraph says is 'easy to misread'; it ignores the point that the neighbourhood is still 'transformed' as poorer households can no longer move in."),
    ("When wealthier people move into a poor neighbourhood, existing tenants are soon driven out by rising rents, which is why such areas change so quickly and so visibly.",
     "This is 'the common fear' that the tracking contradicts, and the paragraph says the change takes 'over a decade without any dramatic eviction'."),
    ("Gentrification displaces poor households less through eviction than through rising rents that steadily push existing tenants out of their homes over the course of a decade.",
     "The paragraph says existing residents leave at rates 'not much higher' than elsewhere; the displacement it describes falls on would-be newcomers, not on existing tenants, so the option misplaces it.")],
   "The paragraph reports a finding that undercuts the fear of eviction, then shows its scope: it measures who leaves, not who arrives, and rising rents exclude poorer newcomers. The correct answer keeps the finding, the shift of focus to arrivals, and the gradual transformation.",
   "Choosing the option about rents pushing tenants out 'over a decade', which borrows the paragraph's timescale and its downplaying of eviction but applies them to the wrong group.",
   "V02H-gentrification-displacement",
   "The subtle distractor has the right mechanism (rents, gradual change) attached to the wrong households; it is separated from the correct answer only by 'not who arrives'.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=D)

# 020 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-020", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Neither our ignorance of future people's wants nor their non-existence frees us from duties to them.",
   "Medium",
   "It is sometimes argued that we owe nothing to people who do not yet exist, since they cannot make claims on us and we cannot know what they will want. The second point is weaker than it looks. We do not know the tastes of people a century from now, but we know with some confidence that they will need breathable air, fertile soil and a climate in which crops grow, just as we do. Obligations of that basic kind do not depend on knowing anyone's particular preferences. And the first point proves too much: a person who buries a hazard that will harm whoever digs there in fifty years seems clearly to wrong that future person, even though no one yet exists to complain.",
   "Neither our ignorance of future people's wants nor their present non-existence frees us from duties to them, since their basic needs are knowable and harming them is plainly wrong.",
   [("We owe nothing to people who do not yet exist, since they cannot make any claims on us and we have no way of knowing what they will want.",
     "This is the argument the paragraph reports in its first sentence and then rebuts point by point."),
    ("We should give the wishes of future generations the same weight as our own, since we can know with confidence what people a century from now will need and prefer.",
     "The paragraph says 'We do not know the tastes of people a century from now'; it claims knowledge only of basic needs, and it says nothing about equal weight."),
    ("Burying a hazard that will harm whoever digs there in fifty years wrongs a future person, even though nobody yet exists to complain about it.",
     "This is the example used against the first point; it omits the reply to the argument from ignorance of future preferences.")],
   "The paragraph rebuts both premises of the argument that we owe future people nothing: ignorance of preferences does not touch basic needs, and the buried-hazard case shows non-existence does not cancel wrongs. The correct answer keeps both replies.",
   "Choosing the option that overstates our knowledge of future people, treating knowledge of basic needs as if it covered preferences.",
   "V02H-duties-to-future-generations",
   "The overreaching distractor sounds like a strong version of the author's view; it fails on the distinction between needs and tastes.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# 021 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-021", "Psychology",
   "Habits form through repetition in a stable setting, so a change of circumstances is a chance to change them.",
   "Easy",
   "People trying to build a habit usually rely on willpower and reminders, but habits form most reliably when a behaviour is repeated in the same setting, at the same time or after the same prompt, until the setting itself begins to trigger it. Once that link is established, the behaviour needs little deliberate effort; the cue does the work. This is why habits are often easiest to change when circumstances change anyway. Someone who moves house or starts a new job has lost many of the old cues at once, and for a while their routines are open to revision in a way they rarely are otherwise.",
   "Habits form when a behaviour is repeated in a stable setting until the setting triggers it, which is why a change of circumstances offers a rare chance to change routines.",
   [("Habits form most reliably when people rely on strong willpower and regular reminders to repeat a behaviour over and over until it no longer requires any deliberate effort.",
     "The paragraph contrasts what people 'usually rely on', willpower and reminders, with what actually works, repetition in 'the same setting'; the option reverses this."),
    ("People who move house or start a new job lose many of their old cues all at once, so their daily routines become open to revision for a while.",
     "This is the illustration in the last sentence; it omits the explanation of how habits form, which is what makes the illustration matter."),
    ("Anyone who wants to change a bad habit should move house or change jobs, since such routines can never be broken while the old surroundings remain.",
     "The paragraph says habits are 'often easiest to change' after a move, not that they 'can never be broken' otherwise, and it recommends no such step.")],
   "The paragraph explains habit formation (repetition in a stable setting until 'the cue does the work') and draws a consequence: lost cues make routines easier to revise. The correct answer states both.",
   "Choosing the illustration about moving house because it is the most concrete sentence.",
   "V02H-habit-formation-context-cues",
   "The mechanism is stated in the first sentence and the consequence is flagged by 'This is why'; distractors are a reversal, a detail and an extreme prescription.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# 022 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-022", "Society and culture",
   "Retirement is hardest for those who relied on work for structure, company and identity, whatever their savings.",
   "Easy",
   "Retirement is usually planned as a financial problem: whether savings will last. For many people the harder adjustment turns out to be of another kind. Work supplies more than income; it gives the week a shape, provides daily contact with people one did not choose but comes to rely on, and answers the question of what one does. Those who retire from jobs that supplied these things, and who have not built other sources of them, can find that a secure income leaves them oddly adrift. People whose sense of themselves was already rooted in family, hobbies or community tend to make the transition more easily, whatever their savings.",
   "Beyond money, retirement removes the structure, company and identity work provided, so it is hardest for those without other sources of these, whatever their savings.",
   [("Financial planning for retirement is pointless, because people with ample savings are just as likely as anyone else to feel adrift once they stop working.",
     "The paragraph says the non-financial adjustment is often 'the harder' one; it never calls financial planning pointless, and it says those with other sources of identity adjust 'more easily'."),
    ("Work gives the week its shape, provides daily contact with colleagues and answers the question of what one does, as well as supplying the income people live on.",
     "This restates the list in the third sentence and leaves out the point about who struggles in retirement and why."),
    ("People with large savings adjust to retirement more easily than others, because a secure income lets them develop new hobbies and ties in their community.",
     "The paragraph says 'a secure income leaves them oddly adrift' and that ease of transition holds 'whatever their savings', which contradicts this option.")],
   "The paragraph compares the financial and the non-financial sides of retirement and argues that the second is often harder, especially for people whose identity rested on work. The correct answer states the comparison and who is most affected.",
   "Choosing the 'pointless' option because the paragraph downplays money, when it only says money is not the whole problem.",
   "V02H-retirement-identity-loss",
   "The point is plain once the phrase 'whatever their savings' is read; the distractors are an overstatement, a list and a contradiction.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# 023 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-023", "History and politics",
   "Dissenters show that a past age's views could be seen past, so the standards of the time lessen blame without removing it.",
   "Difficult",
   "Two responses are common when a celebrated figure from the past is found to have held views now regarded as abhorrent. One insists that such people be judged by the standards of their own time, since no one can be expected to see beyond the moral horizon of their age. The other holds that wrong is wrong, and that admiration should be withdrawn. Both overlook something. Any age contains dissenters, people who did see past the prevailing view, often at some cost, and their existence shows that the horizon was not closed. It does not follow that every figure who failed to join them was a villain; it does follow that 'they were of their time' is a partial excuse, which lessens blame without removing it.",
   "Because dissenters in every age saw past its accepted wrongs, the standards of a figure's own time can lessen blame for abhorrent views but cannot remove it.",
   [("Past figures should be judged only by the moral standards of their own age, since nobody can reasonably be expected to see beyond the outlook of their time.",
     "This is the first common response, which the paragraph says overlooks the dissenters whose existence 'shows that the horizon was not closed'."),
    ("A figure who held views now seen as abhorrent deserves no admiration, since wrong is wrong whatever the standards of the age in which that person lived.",
     "This is the second common response; the paragraph rejects it too, saying it 'does not follow that every figure who failed to join them was a villain'."),
    ("Since some people in every age saw past its prevailing views, those who failed to join these dissenters deserve the same blame as anyone holding such views today.",
     "The paragraph calls the appeal to the time 'a partial excuse, which lessens blame'; 'the same blame' as today denies that it lessens anything.")],
   "The paragraph sets out two opposed responses and rejects both. The existence of dissenters shows the moral horizon was open, so the standards of the time are 'a partial excuse, which lessens blame without removing it'. The correct answer states this middle position and its ground.",
   "Choosing the option that uses the paragraph's own argument about dissenters but draws the stronger conclusion that the excuse counts for nothing.",
   "V02H-judging-past-figures-present-morals",
   "Two distractors are the two views the paragraph rejects, and the third uses its own premise to reach a conclusion it denies; separating them needs the exact force of 'partial excuse'.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=D)

# 024 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-024", "History and politics",
   "The high cost of artillery-proof fortresses shifted power from local lords to tax-raising rulers, without any plan to centralise.",
   "Easy",
   "When cannon became powerful enough to batter down tall stone walls, the castle of a local lord lost much of its value as a refuge. The answer was a new kind of fortification: low, thick ramparts with angled bastions that deflected shot and allowed defenders to cover every approach. Such works were enormously expensive to build and to garrison, far beyond the means of most nobles, and so the ability to hold territory passed increasingly to rulers who could raise taxes across a whole realm. A change in military technology thus did political work, strengthening central authority at the expense of local power, not by any decision to centralise but through the rising cost of defence.",
   "Cannon made castles obsolete, and the costly fortresses replacing them were affordable only to rulers taxing whole realms, so defence costs moved power from lords to the centre.",
   [("The new fortifications had low, thick ramparts and angled bastions, which deflected cannon shot and allowed the defenders to cover every approach to the walls.",
     "This describes the design of the new fortresses from the second sentence and omits the political consequence that the paragraph builds towards."),
    ("Rulers deliberately adopted artillery and expensive new fortresses as part of a long-term plan to centralise their realms and break the power of local nobles.",
     "The paragraph says central authority grew 'not by any decision to centralise but through the rising cost of defence', so a deliberate plan contradicts it."),
    ("Gunpowder weapons ended warfare between local lords altogether, since no noble could any longer build walls that were able to withstand a sustained bombardment.",
     "The paragraph says castles 'lost much of' their value and that power passed 'increasingly' to rulers; it says nothing about warfare between lords ending, so the option overstates and adds.")],
   "The paragraph traces a chain: cannon made tall walls vulnerable, the replacement fortifications were too costly for most nobles, and so holding territory passed to rulers with realm-wide taxes. Its last sentence names the point: technology 'did political work' through cost, not design. The correct answer states the chain and its result.",
   "Assuming that a political outcome must have been intended, and so choosing the option about a deliberate plan.",
   "V02H-artillery-fortresses-state-centralisation",
   "The last sentence states the point and rules out the 'deliberate plan' reading; the other distractors are a detail and an overstatement.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# 025 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ps("V02-H-025", "Economics and business",
   "Network effects make platform leadership durable but not permanent, depending on the cost of using several platforms.",
   "Medium",
   "Platforms that grow more valuable as more people use them, such as messaging services or marketplaces, are often said to be unassailable once they lead: every new user makes the leader more attractive, so challengers cannot catch up. The record is less kind to that claim. Leading platforms have lost their position when a rival offered something the leader could not easily match, or when users could belong to several platforms at once at little cost, so that joining a newcomer did not mean abandoning the old network. Network effects make leadership durable, not permanent; how durable depends largely on how costly it is for users to use more than one platform.",
   "Network effects make a leading platform hard but not impossible to displace, its security depending largely on how costly users find it to join more than one platform.",
   [("Once a platform leads its market, every new user makes it more attractive, so rivals can never catch up with it and its dominance soon becomes effectively permanent.",
     "This is the claim the paragraph says 'The record is less kind to'; its conclusion is that network effects make leadership 'durable, not permanent'."),
    ("Network effects give leading platforms no real protection, since users will always move to whichever new rival offers something the leader cannot easily match.",
     "The paragraph says network effects make leadership 'durable'; 'no real protection' and 'always move' reverse that."),
    ("Regulators should require platforms to let users belong to several networks at once, since this is the only way to keep markets for messaging services competitive.",
     "The paragraph mentions low-cost multiple membership as a condition under which leaders have lost ground; it makes no recommendation to regulators and no 'only way' claim.")],
   "The paragraph critiques the view that leading platforms are unassailable, citing cases where leaders lost ground, and concludes that leadership is 'durable, not permanent', with durability depending on the cost of using several platforms. The correct answer states both parts.",
   "Choosing the regulatory option, which draws a policy from the paragraph's condition rather than stating its point.",
   "V02H-network-effects-platform-lock-in",
   "The reported view and its reversal flank the correct answer, and a plausible policy distractor tempts readers who infer a remedy; the paragraph's final sentence settles it.",
   cognitive="Analysis")
